{"name":"Tarsier","hex":"#825e61","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tarsier","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Used attributively as a specific epithet"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An insectivorous primate of the family Tarsiidae, having very large eyes and long feet, native mainly to several islands of Southeast Asia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tarsier"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tarsier","blocks":[{"text":"Tarsiers are haplorhine primates of the family Tarsiidae, which is the lone extant family within the infraorder Tarsiiformes. Although the group was prehistorically more globally widespread, all of the existing species are native to Maritime Southeast Asia, predominantly in Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia and the Philippines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Philippine_tarsier_%28Carlito_syrichta_fraterculus%29_Bohol_2.jpg/330px-Philippine_tarsier_%28Carlito_syrichta_fraterculus%29_Bohol_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarsier"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Combined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg/500px-Combined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Combined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg/1280px-Combined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Combined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg/500px-Combined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACombined_Range_of_9_Tarsier_Species.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Tarsier_%28Inside_Philippine_National_Mu